    imageHint: "Slutsk museum building and traditional woven belts",
    descriptionAdvanced: {
      de: "Das historische Zentrum von Sluzk ist untrennbar mit einer der prächtigsten handwerklichen Traditionen der belarussischen Geschichte verbunden: der Herstellung der weltberühmten Sluzker Gürtel. Die Stadt, die bereits 1116 in historischen Chroniken erwähnt wurde, erlebte im 18. Jahrhundert unter der Schirmherrschaft der Radziwiłł-Fürsten eine kulturelle und wirtschaftliche Blütezeit. In der 1758 gegründeten Textilmanufaktur, der 'Persiarnya', wurden aufwendige, bis zu vier Meter lange Seidengürtel gewebt, die mit echten Gold- und Silberfäden durchwirkt waren. Diese Gürtel waren ein unverzichtbares und teures Statussymbol des polnisch-litauischen Adels (Szlachta) und verkörperten Reichtum und elitäre Zugehörigkeit. Obwohl die ursprüngliche Produktion im 19. Jahrhundert zum Erliegen kam, hält Sluzk dieses einzigartige Erbe lebendig. Das moderne Museum der Sluzker Gürtel präsentiert nicht nur historische Originale, sondern demonstriert auch die Wiederbelebung dieses filigranen Handwerks an speziellen Webstühlen, die das Erbe in die Gegenwart tragen.",
      hu: "Szluck történelmi központja elválaszthatatlanul összefonódik a fehérorosz történelem egyik legpompásabb kézműves hagyományával: a világhírű szlucki övek készítésével. A város, amelyet már 1116-ban említettek a történelmi krónikák, a 18. században a Radziwiłł hercegek pártfogása alatt kulturális és gazdasági virágzását élte. Az 1758-ban alapított 'Persiarnya' nevű textilmanufaktúrában bonyolult, akár négy méter hosszú selyemöveket szőttek, amelyeket valódi arany- és ezüstszálakkal átszőttek. Ezek az övek a lengyel-litván nemesség (Szlachta) nélkülözhetetlen és drága státuszszimbólumai voltak, amelyek a gazdagságot és az elithez való tartozást testesítették meg. Bár az eredeti termelés a 19. században megszűnt, Szluck életben tartja ezt az egyedülálló örökséget. A modern Szlucki Övek Múzeuma nemcsak a történelmi eredetieket mutatja be, hanem demonstrálja e kifinomult kézműves mesterség újjáélesztését is olyan speciális szövőszékeken, amelyek a hagyományt a jelenbe viszik át.",
      ro: "Centrul istoric al orașului Slutsk este inseparabil legat de una dintre cele mai somptuoase tradiții meșteșugărești din istoria belarusă: producția faimoaselor cingători de Slutsk. Orașul, menționat în cronicile istorice încă din 1116, a cunoscut în secolul al XVIII-lea o perioadă de înflorire culturală și economică sub patronajul prinților Radziwiłł. În manufactura textilă 'Persiarnya', înființată în 1758, se țeseau cingători complexe de mătase, lungi de până la patru metri, care erau intercalate cu fire de aur și argint veritabil. Aceste cingători reprezentau un simbol de statut indispensabil și scump al nobilimii polono-lituaniene (Szlachta) și întruchipau bogăția și apartenența la elită. Deși producția originală a încetat în secolul al XIX-lea, Slutsk păstrează vie această moștenire unică. Muzeul modern al Cingătorilor de Slutsk nu numai că prezintă originale istorice, dar demonstrează și renașterea acestui meșteșug filigranat la războaie de țesut speciale, care duc tradiția în prezent.",
      en: "",
//       en: "The historic center of Slutsk is inseparably linked to one of the most magnificent craft traditions in Eastern Europe: the making of the famous Slutsk belts. These masterfully woven sashes, wide and up to four meters long, made of precious silk, gold, and silver threads, were the ultimate status symbol for the Polish-Lithuanian nobility in the 18th century. The golden age of the local textile industry began when the Radziwiłł magnate family brought Armenian and Persian master weavers to Slutsk starting in 1758 to establish a large manufactory. Subsequently, a unique local style emerged that seamlessly blended Eastern ornaments with traditional Belarusian floral motifs. Although the original production ceased in the 19th century, today the Museum of Slutsk Belts keeps this cultural heritage alive, where visitors can admire original belts and watch modern, computer-controlled looms recreating the historical patterns.",
//       en: "The historic center of Slutsk is inseparably linked to one of the most magnificent artisanal traditions in Belarusian history: the production of the world-famous Slutsk belts. The town, which was mentioned in historical chronicles as early as 1116, experienced a cultural and economic golden age in the 18th century under the patronage of the Radziwiłł princes. In the textile manufactory known as 'Persiarnya', founded in 1758, complex silk belts up to four meters long were woven, intricately interlaced with real gold and silver threads. These belts were an indispensable and expensive status symbol of the Polish-Lithuanian nobility (Szlachta), embodying wealth and elite belonging. Although the original production ceased in the 19th century, Slutsk keeps this unique heritage alive. The modern Museum of Slutsk Belts not only displays historical originals but also demonstrates the revival of this delicate craft on special looms that bring the heritage into the present."
    },
